-- Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two, four amphibious and aquatic vehicles (printed 177-186): Alligator APC, Aquapod with its Geo-Explorer mini-sub, Beachcomber ATV, Bluestorm submarine
-- 4 vehicle rows, with 44 locations and 15 weapon entries.
--
-- One-off data script, run once per environment. NOT a migration - it adds
-- rows, not schema.
--
--   node scripts/d1-apply.mjs --local apps/character-creator/db/~318-northern-gun-2-aquatic-a.sql
--
-- Written by scripts/vessel-sql.mjs from 1 worker file(s). Every value
-- is printable ASCII, every row cites its book, and a Main Body location
-- agrees with its vehicle's mdc_main_body.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO vehicles (slug, name, system, vehicle_class, crew, passengers, speed_ground, speed_air, speed_water, dimensions, weight_tons, mdc_main_body, is_mega_damage, ar, cost, cost_note, description, source_book) VALUES
  ('ng-amp-007-alligator-apc', 'NG-AMP-007 Alligator Amphibious APC', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'Two: a driver and a gunner.', '10-16 troops in body armor or 6-8 in power armor; 20 if crammed in.', '75 mph (120 km) maximum; usually cruises at 30-40 mph (48 to 64 km) in wilderness.', NULL, '40 mph (64 km/34.7 knots) maximum, riding on the surface. Not a submersible.', 'Height 9 ft (2.7 m); width 10 ft (3 m); length 32 ft (9.7 m)', '20 tons', 345, 1, NULL, 1800000, '3.2 million credits nuclear (about 12 years), 2.4 million Solid Oxide, 1.8 million electric battery; the lowest is stored.', 'Model NG-AMP-007 (Class: Amphibious Armored Personnel Carrier). A sealed troop carrier that crosses water as a boat, then rolls up the beach on six wheels and keeps fighting as a light armoured APC, landing one or two squads at the shore or carrying them well inland. Its lead designers, Allan Fierro, Michael Ong and Jeff Reade, meant it for more than landings, and it does harbor and coastal work of every kind: ship-to-shore, shore-to-ship and ship-to-ship transfers, patrols on land or water and chases across the line between them, putting troops into and pulling them out of hot zones, rescues, supply drops, cargo and fire support. It often goes in with the Crocodile amphibious tank, the Beachmaster robot and Beach-Stormer power armor; navies, pirates, privateers and shore towns pair it with the Crocodile for raids and coastal defence, and mercenaries and adventurers in river, lake and marsh country use both for exploration. Built to need little upkeep in the field and to shrug off corrosion from water, swamp and mud. Tires: one lost of the six changes nothing; two lost on one side cost -20% piloting for evasive or trick driving and above 40 mph (64 km), and a failed roll then has a 01-55% chance to tip it onto its side. Cargo: four internal lockers holding two rifles, four pistols, two spare E-Clips per weapon, two Vibro-Knives, six canteens and a survival kit. Sensors: standard, as robot vehicles.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.177-178'),
  ('ng-aqua-212-aquapod', 'NG-AQUA-212 Aquapod with Geo-Explorer Mini-Sub', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'Aquapod: three, a pilot, a co-pilot/communications officer and a gunner. Mini-sub: two, a pilot and a copilot; often a separate crew so both craft can act at once.', 'Aquapod: six seated forward, and 6-12 more standing on the open top only at anchor in calm water or under 15 mph (24 km). Mini-sub: up to six, eight cramped.', 'No wheels. Hovers, VTOL, at up to 55 mph (88 km), 75 mph (120 km) with the sub away and the bay empty; cruises at 40 mph (64 km). Rides 4-25 ft (1.2 to 7.6 m) above the ground and handles drops of up to 200 ft (61 m). Parks on five 4 ft (1.2 m) landing struts.', NULL, 'Aquapod: surface 55 mph (88 km or 47.8 knots); it cannot submerge. Mini-sub: surface 25 mph (40 km/21.7 knots), underwater 20 mph (32 km/17.3 knots), maximum depth 2 miles (3.2 km).', 'Aquapod: height 14 ft (4.3 m) with the tail fins; width 30 ft (9.1 m) wing tip to wing tip, a 16 ft body and 14 ft of wings; length 40 ft (12.2 m), 45 ft (13.7 m) with the sub docked. Mini-sub: height 11 ft (3.3 m), width 8 ft (2.4 m), length 24 ft (7.3 m)', 'Aquapod 7 tons empty, without sub or cargo; mini-sub 4 tons fully loaded', 330, 1, NULL, 4800000, '4.8 million credits for the Aquapod with its mini-sub; 2.1 million for the Aquapod alone, docking mechanism included; 2.7 million for the mini-sub alone, which then needs a charging station of some kind. A second nose laser turret is 500,000 more.', 'Model NG-AQUA-212 (Class: Multi-Terrain Boat and Land Hover Vehicle with Deep Water Exploration Capabilities), nicknamed Leap Frog for hopping between bodies of water and Flying Flounder for its flat, wide shape. A surface craft, never itself a submersible, that runs on water like a fast boat and floats over land like a hover truck, carrying the AQUA-212MS Geo-Explorer mini-submarine locked between its two main thruster arms. Scott Goric, Jeff Peterson and Aaron Nelson had to fight for it; it now sells steadily to salvagers, treasure hunters, privateers and explorers, and lately in Mexico to vampire hunters, who value a craft that can anchor offshore, slip about quietly powered down, and launch a sub, and NG has sent salespeople south. With the sub out, the docking bay carries 10 tons, four cars, 12-14 hovercycles or 10 pallets (20 stacked two high), but the sub is stranded until the Aquapod returns, and it can enter the water only from an Aquapod on the surface. Aquapod: crew forward with seats for six, four bunks, a lavatory and water for up to 9 people for 10-14 days; two 4x4x7 ft (1.2 x 1.2 x 2.1 m) bays of six tons each (the prose says eight) and six 2x2x4 ft lockers. Its shape cannot be disguised and it is slow on land. Losing one main thruster halves speed and height and costs -15% piloting; every window has a sliding 50 M.D.C. plate that seals it once the glass loses half its M.D.C.; range is unlimited on its nuclear supply (about 12 years) but the hover jets cool for 30 minutes after eight hours. Mini-sub: battery charged off the Aquapod in 8 hours, good for about 24 hours or 200 miles (320 km), up to seven days drifting surfaced or resting on the bottom, plus a 72-hour backup for lights and life support only; food and water for 6-10 days; lockers of SCUBA gear, tanks and wet suits. Its sensor package lost, the pilots have only their own senses; its screw lost, it must surface and drift or be towed; window plates have 50 M.D.C., porthole plates 30. Sub sensors: thermal imager and three cameras (telescopic and macro lenses) to 2,000 ft (610 m), infrared and ultraviolet optics, three nose searchlights and two infrared searchlights to 500 ft (152 m), sonar tracking 64 targets to 50 miles (80 km), and a translator holding the nine known languages of the Americas with room for 12 more. Aquapod sensors: standard, as robot vehicles. The width line''s metric figures (7.3 m for 16 ft, 4.9 m for 14 ft) do not convert.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.179-182'),
  ('ng-amp6-beachcomber-atv', 'NG-AMP6 Beachcomber Amphibious ATV', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'One pilot/driver, who is also the gunner.', 'Up to 8.', '80 mph (128 km) maximum; only 10% slower in mud or snow, and handles well on ice below 40 mph (64 km).', NULL, '40 mph (64 km) maximum.', 'Height 8 ft (2.4 m); width 9 ft (2.7 m); length 28 ft (8.5 m)', '8 tons', 190, 1, NULL, 350000, '975,000 credits nuclear (about 12 years), 700,000 Solid Oxide, 350,000 electric battery or liquid fuel; the lowest is stored. Range 500 miles (800 km) on battery, 400 miles (640 km) on fuel. The optional NG-VM8 launcher is 97,500 more.', 'Model NG-AMP6 (Class: Amphibious All-Terrain Vehicle). Half ATV and half boat, from a team led by Jeffrey Martin, Magnar Laksa and Brian Lindholm: a hulled truck in the mould of military amphibious tractors, made for explorers and wanderers, that treats rivers, flooded roads, swamps, lakes and even open sea as passable with a team of 8-10 adventurers or marines aboard and a few guns for defence. Built for toughness, with a first-rate all-terrain drive, oversized tires whose pressure adjusts to the ground, a winch as standard and hydraulic suspension that raises or lowers the body; its way with mud, snow and ice has made it a favourite in Michigan, the Midwest and Canada. Main body gone: it shuts down, and afloat it sinks within 3D6 minutes. Cargo: a 4x6x2 ft (1.2 x 1.8 x 0.6 m) compartment at the back, about 2 tons besides the passengers. Power: nuclear, Solid Oxide, electric battery or combustion; the fuel and battery models carry an NG-202 rail gun in the turret in place of the laser. Sensors: standard, as robot vehicles.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.183-184'),
  ('ng-su2-bluestorm-submarine', 'NG-SU2 Bluestorm Submarine', 'rifts', 'ship', 'Five: a pilot, a copilot/sonar operator, two gunners and a torpedo operator, with room for two more crew. Someone must be at the torpedo tube to load and fire it.', 'Up to ten. Ishpeming Navy boats carry 10 naval commandos or special forces, half that in power armor.', NULL, NULL, 'Surface 32 mph (51 km); underwater 40 mph (64 km), maximum depth 3 miles (4.8 km). Dives in about 30 seconds (2 melee rounds).', 'Height 20 ft (6 m); width 24 ft (7 m); length 54 ft (16.5 m)', '62 tons', 300, 1, NULL, 38000000, 'Not sold on the open market. To allied nations: 42 million credits nuclear (about 15 years) or 38 million Solid Oxide; the lowest is stored.', 'Model NG-SU2 (Class: Special Operations Submersible). Northern Gun''s first military-grade submarine, designed from scratch rather than copied like its generic submersibles (Rifts Underseas): a small, quiet ambush boat to guard the coasts, river mouths and shipping lanes, and a base from which to put special forces ashore. Medium torpedoes deal with big ships and sea monsters, lasers serve as deck guns, and it holds a squad and enough stores for long patrols or supply drops. Its trick is an anti-aircraft missile pod let out up to 2 miles (3.2 km) on a light polymer line that carries data both ways, so a gunner aboard can fire on helicopters, flying monsters, power armor, sub-hunting aircraft or ships while the enemy hunts for the pod instead of the boat. Only the Ishpeming military and NG''s allies, the Manistique Imperium and the Coalition States, may have it; pirates and privateers covet it. Rear dive hatch: takes two divers, or a single trooper in power armor, at once, and puts a pair out every four melee rounds (one minute). Losing more than one dive plane makes it hard to change depth: torpedoes at it are +1 to strike and diving takes a full minute (4 melee rounds). Main body gone: it shuts down, and afloat it sinks within 4D6 minutes. Endurance: six months of food and supplies with the holds empty; missions usually run under half that. Cargo: 8 tons in the bay and 20 more on the flat deck, which halves speed and wrecks or loses anything not prepared for submersion. Sensors: standard, as robot vehicles, plus passive sonar tracking 30 targets to 10 miles (16 km) unnoticed and active sonar to 50 miles (80 km), which gives its position away.', 'Rifts World Book 34: Northern Gun Two p.184-186');

